==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|polyseme|y}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɒ.lɪ.siː.mi/|/pəˈlɪ.sɪ.mi/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-polysemy.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-polysemy2.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|+}}

# {{lb|en|semantics}} The [[quality]] characteristic of a [[polyseme]], a [[sign]] (such as a [[word]] or [[symbol]]) that has [[multiple]] [[meaning]]s ([[sense#English:_any particular meaning of a word|senses]]), often including multiple similar ones.
#: {{ant|en|monosemy}}
#: {{hyper|en|colexification}}
#: {{hypo|en|q=linear types|autohyponymy|autohypernymy|autosuperordination|automeronymy|autoholonymy}}
#: {{cot|en|homonymy|;|homosemy}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Polysemy: Flexible Patterns of Meaning in Mind and Language|page=3|author=Brigitte Nerlich|year=2011|passage=Fifty years ago the linguist and semanticist Stephen Ullmann wrote that '''polysemy''', the fact that some words have a network of multiple but related meanings, is "the pivot of semantic analysis" […]}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2017-07-27|author=Agustín Vicente and Ingrid L. Falkum|title=Polysemy|journal=Oxford Research Encyclopedia of Linguistics|publisher=Oxford University Press|url=https://oxfordre.com/linguistics/display/10.1093/acrefore/9780199384655.001.0001/acrefore-9780199384655-e-325|passage=Polysemy proliferates in natural language: Virtually every word is polysemous to some extent.}}

